WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.200
The 2008 horror film "The Strangers"&nbsp;
has become a disturbing classic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.040
and an excellent example of the&nbsp;
home invasion genre of horror.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:14.720
Lock the door and join us as we explore the&nbsp;
meaning of the film's dark, nihilistic ending.

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:20.720
Kristen and James ride in uncomfortable&nbsp;
silence. It's a cool night in February,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:24.480
and the pair are making their way from&nbsp;
a friend's wedding reception to James'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:29.280
summer home. James was so sure that Kristen&nbsp;
was going to accept his marriage proposal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:33.200
Kristen's tear-stained face says&nbsp;
it all: Where do they go from here?

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:36.480
There's a knock on the door. A&nbsp;
blonde woman, bathed in shadow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:40.800
asks for Tamara. After an awkward&nbsp;
exchange, they send her on her way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:45.200
James attempts to gather his thoughts&nbsp;
and leaves on a night drive, leaving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:50.901
Kristen alone in the house. Then, suddenly,&nbsp;
another knock. More insistent this time.

00:00:50.901 --> 00:00:52.758
[Knocking on the door]

00:00:56.983 --> 00:00:58.401
[Loud bang]

00:00:58.401 --> 00:01:02.400
Kristen grabs a knife and starts&nbsp;
charging her phone. The landline is dead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:06.160
and she can't shake the feeling&nbsp;
that someone is in the house.

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:11.600
So begins "The Strangers," which&nbsp;
barrels forward with unrelenting energy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:17.840
pitting the emotionally strained couple against&nbsp;
three masked assailants. James returns to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:22.640
Kristen a mess, and he soon finds out&nbsp;
why. Together they attempt to survive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:28.320
in their own home, defend themselves,&nbsp;
and call for help, all to no avail.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:33.680
At the end of their terrifying night, James&nbsp;
is missing and Kristen is badly injured.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:38.560
Her attempts to signal for help from a remote&nbsp;
radio are a bust, and upon returning to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:43.600
house the power cuts out. Limping and&nbsp;
surrounded by the masked intruders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:49.680
Kristen attempts to find James, only to be knocked&nbsp;
out by the towering male in the group. She awakens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:56.000
tied to a chair next to James, distraught&nbsp;
and defeated with one searing question: why?

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:00.480
Kristen wakes up to the sound of&nbsp;
Pin-Up Girl opening the drapes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:05.680
Warm sunlight floods the room. Kristen&nbsp;
grunts against her tight restraints, kicking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:11.520
and whimpering as her three masked assailants&nbsp;
stand silently over her and the defeated James.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:18.080
We tend to expect horrific things to take place&nbsp;
under the cover of darkness; things that slink,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:23.200
creep, and strike while we're&nbsp;
sleepy, vulnerable, and alone.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:29.440
Evolutionary psychologists have theorized that our&nbsp;
fear of the dark lingers from the days when human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:35.040
beings weren't at the top of the food chain,&nbsp;
when nocturnal predators were a real threat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:38.800
Horror films take advantage of&nbsp;
our instinctual fear of the dark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:44.640
allowing our imaginations to run wild&nbsp;
and conjure up worst-case scenarios.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:50.400
In genre cinema, horrific things tend to lurk&nbsp;
in the shadows. That said, there's no shortage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:56.000
of horror film baddies that work in the light.&nbsp;
The man-eating shark in "Jaws" didn't confine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:02.080
its reign of terror to the shadows and neither&nbsp;
do the trio of killers in "The Strangers." And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:08.720
this is precisely what makes the climax&nbsp;
of their murderous assault so disturbing.

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:13.440
They open the curtains before&nbsp;
killing Kristen and James, unafraid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:17.280
and unconcerned with the possibility&nbsp;
that some prying neighbor might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:25.840
see. The fact that "The Strangers" ends bathed&nbsp;
in sunlight is both unexpected and terrifying;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:31.200
further cementing the film's core theme&nbsp;
that random acts of violence are exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:37.040
that: random. This isn't the kind of evil&nbsp;
that is miraculously banished by the sun.

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:42.720
There are few glimmers of hope throughout&nbsp;
"The Strangers." One of them is when Mike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:45.760
(played by Dennis Reynolds&nbsp;
himself, Glenn Howerton)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:51.040
arrives at the house in response to James' upset&nbsp;
voice message. Mike's a good pal. His friend's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:56.480
marriage proposal fell through and here he is&nbsp;
at god knows what hour to pick up the pieces.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:01.040
Unfortunately, Mike's good intentions lead&nbsp;
to his demise. Waltzing into the house,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:05.920
oblivious of the situation, he's shot in&nbsp;
the face by James' itchy trigger finger.

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:10.960
To be fair, when a person blows their best&nbsp;
friend's face off, the first reaction isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:16.000
typically to steal his cell phone. Even&nbsp;
as an audience member, it's easy to forget&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:20.960
that Mike has a phone that could be Kristen&nbsp;
and James' chance to contact the police.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:25.600
After the incident, James announces that&nbsp;
he's going to try and call for help — not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:31.760
by checking his dead friend's pockets — but by&nbsp;
venturing out for the two-way radio in the woods.

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:37.200
To be fair, the movie wants us to forget that Mike&nbsp;
has a cell phone, so that it can make a dramatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:42.160
appearance in the final act. Bleeding out on&nbsp;
the floor, Kristen hears Mike's phone ring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:44.640
something we didn't even realize was there.

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:48.240
Upon coming-to, lashed to a chair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:52.880
Kristen breathlessly asks the trio&nbsp;
what we've all been wondering:

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:59.120
"Why are you doing this to us?"
"Because you were home."]

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:04.000
This attack has nothing to do with&nbsp;
Kristen and James' relationship struggles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:06.240
with the apparent wealth of James' family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:12.560
or with any sinister occult purpose. They answered&nbsp;
the door. It's just that random and simple.

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:17.920
Indeed, the scariest thing about "The&nbsp;
Strangers" isn't the masks, the jump scares,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:24.240
or the bloodshed. Instead, the film offers a&nbsp;
glimpse into the terrifying notion that sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:30.880
bad things happen ... just because. There's&nbsp;
no grand reveal and no explanation for our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:35.200
villains' actions. When the assailants&nbsp;
remove their masks, Kristen and James&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:41.760
don't recognize their faces. The killers are,&nbsp;
as the film title suggests, just strangers.

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:49.040
This hits especially hard in these final moments&nbsp;
— even at the film's end, there are no answers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:54.960
All we're left with is pure dread. Because we&nbsp;
know so little about these anonymous boogeymen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.960 --> 00:06:00.480
they are able to remain just that: Something&nbsp;
that can go bump in the night without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:05.840
any warning or purpose. The conclusion&nbsp;
isn't here to satisfy your curiosity.

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:12.800
Satisfied with the damage they've inflicted,&nbsp;
our now unmasked trio exit James' summer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:18.080
house and pile into their pickup. Their truck&nbsp;
passes two Mormon boys, walking their bikes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:22.560
on the side of the road. They stop and chat&nbsp;
with the boys, then continue down the road.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:27.120
As they drive away, Pin-Up Girl makes a&nbsp;
curious comment to Dollface. She says:

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:30.560
"It'll be easier next time."

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:36.880
The internet loves theorizing, and passionate&nbsp;
Redditors believe Pin-Up's comment may imply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:41.280
that the events we see in the film were&nbsp;
an initiation rite of sorts for Dollface.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:46.880
Indeed, if you look at the trio's masks and their&nbsp;
credited names, a sort of hierarchy emerges.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:53.280
Man in Mask never speaks; he’s a dominating&nbsp;
presence who towers over his two smaller peers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:56.800
Pin-Up Girl always appears&nbsp;
assured and well-practiced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.800 --> 00:07:02.560
with a name suggesting a degree of maturity.&nbsp;
Dollface, meanwhile, is another story;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:09.120
a weak voice beneath a wide-eyed childish&nbsp;
mask. It's possible that she's new to this.

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:12.800
This interpretation potentially&nbsp;
puts Pin-Up Girl's comment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:18.480
that it will be easier next time, into context.&nbsp;
Dollface's struggle with the acts she’s committed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:22.720
is further implied by her interactions&nbsp;
with the Mormon boys. She's a sinner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:28.000
and she's a little shaken up by what she's just&nbsp;
done. But next time, it won't be her first rodeo.

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:32.640
The idea for "The Strangers" came&nbsp;
from a number of different real-life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:37.200
sources. While you have to go digging through&nbsp;
the film's bonus features and production notes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:42.400
to uncover how writer-director Bryan Bertino's&nbsp;
own brush with home invasion inspired the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:47.040
the ending of "The Strangers" hammers home&nbsp;
some of the film's more well-known sources.

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:52.240
As our unmasked trio pile into their pickup&nbsp;
truck, the connective tissue to the famous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:58.000
Tate–LaBianca murders is unavoidable. As Bertino&nbsp;
recounted in an interview, the film was indeed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:02.800
in direct conversation with the murders enacted&nbsp;
by the Manson Family. Bertino explained that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:07.840
learning about the story of Charles Manson and his&nbsp;
followers, along with the group's seeming lack of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:14.000
any singular motivation, had a massive impact on&nbsp;
him and his understanding of the minds of killers.

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:18.400
Writer Britt Hayes notes another source of&nbsp;
inspiration for the film. This incident is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:22.560
known as the Keddie Cabin Murders, an&nbsp;
unsolved quadruple homicide that took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:28.560
place in a California railroad town in 1981.&nbsp;
One of the most unnerving similarities between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:33.280
"The Strangers" and the Keddie Cabin Murders&nbsp;
takes place during the film's conclusion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:38.080
With Kristen and James bound to chairs in their&nbsp;
living room and stabbed in the early morning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:42.640
the scene bears a strong resemblance to&nbsp;
the fates of some of the case's victims.

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:50.000
Love 'em or hate 'em, jump scares are an essential&nbsp;
part of horror films. While not all screamers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:54.880
as they’re sometimes called, are created&nbsp;
equal, some are nobler than others.

00:08:54.880 --> 00:08:58.640
The word "stingers" describes the&nbsp;
jump scare that gives the audience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:02.640
one last jolt before the credits&nbsp;
roll — and believe it or not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:07.600
it's a time-honored tradition. There's the dream&nbsp;
sequence at the end of "Carrie," for instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:13.440
where our witchy heroine's bloodied arm bursts&nbsp;
out of her rocky grave to snatch the traumatized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:19.520
Sue. And who could forget the final shot of&nbsp;
the franchise-originator "Friday the 13th,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:24.240
when Jason's decomposing corpse springs&nbsp;
out of Crystal Lake for one final shock?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:30.000
A final jump scare is a totally acceptable&nbsp;
(if a bit silly) way to end a horror film.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:35.680
"The Strangers" keeps that tradition alive.&nbsp;
As the two Mormon boys (who are going to need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:42.400
some serious therapy after this) venture into the&nbsp;
house, they spy James and Kristen's mangled bodies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:48.160
and investigate. Hovering over Kristen, the&nbsp;
boys take in the crime scene with a frankly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:53.680
eerie silence, dumbstruck perhaps at&nbsp;
the gruesome bloodbath before them.

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:58.800
One boy hovers over Kristen's body,&nbsp;
extending his hand towards her face.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:02.160
Then: bam. Kristen comes&nbsp;
to, screaming. Cut to black.

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:07.280
Considering the film's nihilistic attitude,&nbsp;
Kristen's survival is initially surprising.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:13.760
But upon closer inspection, the assailants leaving&nbsp;
her alive gives us some disturbing insight into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.760 --> 00:10:21.360
the killers' psyche. They are not concerned with&nbsp;
the act of killing, or even being caught. All that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:27.360
interests them is the violence — rhyme,&nbsp;
reason, or consequence be damned.

